]

Examples include:

* spatial geometry;
* symmetry;
* oscillation;
* tracers;
* phenomenal boundaries;
* attentional flow;
* cessation;
* valence magnitude.

## Method 3 — Treat unusual states as natural interventions

QRI considers exotic states valuable because they reveal ranges of phenomenal organisation hidden by ordinary waking stability.

The scientific logic is sound in principle:

[
\text{perturb system}
\rightarrow
\text{observe structured phenomenal change}
\rightarrow
\text{infer latent mechanism}.
]

The difficulty is obtaining precise, reproducible and minimally theory-contaminated reports.

## Method 4 — Phenomenological replication

Instead of accepting verbal reports alone, QRI develops:

* visual simulations;
* tactile visualisations;
* geometric replications;
* interactive psychophysics tools;
* computational models.

The researcher asks:

> Can a proposed mathematical or physical process reproduce the structure of the reported experience?

This turns phenomenology into model comparison rather than literary description.

## Method 5 — Transfer mathematics from physics into phenomenology

QRI deliberately imports:

* group symmetry;
* topology;
* harmonic analysis;
* eigenmodes;
* wave mechanics;
* nonlinear optics;
* coupling kernels;
* Fourier transforms;
* conservation principles.

The strong-monist justification is that physical and phenomenal laws should be structurally related if they describe two projections of one reality.

## Method 6 — Use impossible cases to eliminate theories

The slicing and boundary problems do not immediately prove QRI’s field theory.

They constrain the space of possible theories.

A valid theory must answer:

* How many subjects exist?
* Where does binding stop?
* What physical change constitutes division?
* Why is a concrete token rather than an abstract type conscious?

This adversarial method is among QRI’s strongest contributions.

## Method 7 — Use simulations before full measurement becomes possible

QRI frequently builds generative models of:

* field topology;
* cessation transitions;
* coupling kernels;
* wave propagation;
* altered geometries.

The simulations do not establish that the brain implements the model.

They clarify what the model predicts and what future measurements would need to detect.

