# 23. The ten unresolved liabilities

## 1. The privileged-formalism problem

Many mathematical objects can encode the same experience.

Why is one representation ontologically privileged?

## 2. The symmetry problem

A structure may be symmetrical under one representation or transformation group and asymmetrical under another.

STV requires the correct object and group before symmetry becomes meaningful.

## 3. The manifestation problem

Even perfect topological segmentation does not explain why a pocket is phenomenally present:

[
\operatorname{Pocket}(P_i)
\not\Rightarrow_{\mathrm{proven}}
D_i.
]

QRI usually answers through panpsychism or dual-aspect identity.

That is a metaphysical postulate.

## 4. The overgeneration problem

Physical fields contain countless transient topological structures.

Which pockets are subjects?

## 5. The scale problem

QRI has not yet measured the proposed consciousness-individuating topology in a human brain.

## 6. The causal-loop problem

It must demonstrate that the proposed field pattern influences neural processing in the required holistic manner rather than merely accompanying it.

## 7. The content-mapping problem

Topology may identify boundaries without explaining why the field contains red, pain, space or thought.

